  <PuppyFoodCalc slot="calculator" />

  <h2>How to use the puppy food calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Weigh your puppy on a kitchen or baby scale. Weighing yourself holding the puppy and subtracting also works.</li>
    <li>Enter the weight, your puppy’s age, and the expected adult size class or weight.</li>
    <li>Enter the kcal per cup from your food’s label. This is listed as “kcal/cup” or “ME” in the guaranteed analysis area.</li>
    <li>Read the daily calorie estimate and the cup range, then divide the total across meals.</li>
    <li>Re-check body condition and weight every one to two weeks and rerun the calculator.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How the estimate works</h2>
  <p>
    The calculation starts with Resting Energy Requirement, a standard veterinary estimate of the
    calories a dog burns at rest: <code>RER = 70 × (body weight in kg)^0.75</code>. The exponent
    reflects the fact that metabolism does not scale in a straight line with weight; a 20 kg dog does
    not need twice the calories of a 10 kg dog.
  </p>
  <p>
    RER is then multiplied by a life-stage factor. Commonly taught values for growing dogs are about
    <strong>3.0 × RER up to four months of age</strong> and about <strong>2.0 × RER from four months
    to adulthood</strong>. These come from the NRC and veterinary nutrition teaching and are starting
    points, not rules. Active, lean, or fast-growing puppies may need more; calm or chubby ones less.
  </p>

  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    A 5 kg (11 lb) puppy at 12 weeks: RER = 70 × 5^0.75 ≈ 234 kcal. At 3.0 × RER the estimate is about
    <strong>700 kcal per day</strong>. If the food contains 400 kcal per cup, that is about
    <strong>1.75 cups per day</strong>, or just under half a cup at each of four meals. Two months
    later the same puppy might weigh 9 kg; RER is about 365 kcal, and at 2.0 × RER the target is about
    730 kcal. Notice that the daily calories barely changed even though the puppy gained weight,
    because the growth multiplier fell. This is why a feeding amount that worked at 12 weeks can
    quietly turn into overfeeding at 16 weeks.
  </p>

  <h2>Typical puppy feeding frequency</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Age</th><th>Meals per day</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>6–12 weeks</td><td>4</td><td>Small stomachs; toy breeds may need an extra snack to avoid low blood sugar.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3–6 months</td><td>3</td><td>Keep meal times consistent to help with house training.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6–12 months</td><td>2–3</td><td>Move to two meals as appetite and routine settle.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>12+ months</td><td>2</td><td>Transition to adult food by breed size, gradually.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Adjusting for body condition</h2>
  <p>
    Calories are only half of the picture. The most reliable check is hands-on: you should be able to
    feel your puppy’s ribs under a thin layer of padding, and see a waist from above. If ribs are hard
    to find, reduce the daily amount by about 10 percent and reassess in two weeks. If ribs are
    sharply visible, increase by 10 percent and speak with your vet, because parasites and other
    health problems can also cause a thin puppy. Our <a href="/tools/dog-bmi-calculator/">dog body
    condition helper</a> and the WSAVA 1–9 scale describe what to look for.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Feeding adult-food amounts to a puppy, or puppy-food amounts to a nearly grown dog.</li>
    <li>Ignoring treats, chews, and food used for training.</li>
    <li>Using cups from a different brand — kcal per cup can range from under 300 to over 500.</li>
    <li>Free-feeding large-breed puppies, which makes rapid growth more likely.</li>
    <li>Changing foods suddenly instead of mixing old and new over seven or more days.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Related reading</h2>
  <p>
    For a broader overview read <a href="/guides/how-much-to-feed-a-puppy/">How much to feed a puppy</a>.
    To plan ahead, use the <a href="/tools/puppy-weight-calculator/">puppy weight calculator</a> to
    estimate adult size, and the <a href="/tools/dog-food-calculator/">dog food calculator</a> once your
    dog is fully grown. If you prepare meals at home, see the{' '}
    <a href="/tools/homemade-dog-food-calculator/">homemade dog food calculator</a> and talk to a
    veterinary nutritionist, since growing puppies are especially sensitive to calcium and phosphorus
    imbalances.
  </p>
